5, -0.010101318359375, -0.021514892578125, 0.035552978515625, 0.022064208984375, 0.00611114501953125, -0.03277587890625, 0.0633544921875, 0.020111083984375, 0.0124053955078125, -0.04949951171875, -0.00820159912109375, 0.00954437255859375, -0.0209197998046875, 0.0009870529174804688, -0.04046630859375, 0.0186920166015625, -0.052215576171875, 0.0043792724609375, -0.037933349609375, -0.0249786376953125, 0.01284027099609375, -0.04669189453125, -0.0159759521484375, -0.011688232421875, 0.011688232421875, -0.00748443603515625, 0.0161590576171875, -0.01110076904296875, -0.011383056640625, -0.006916046142578125, -0.036865234375, -0.012664794921875, 0.019683837890625, -0.01425933837890625, -0.0145263671875, -0.00011849403381347656, 0.03973388671875, 0.0303955078125, -0.003662109375, 0.0023345947265625, -0.039520263671875, 0.00325775146484375, -0.00013744831085205078, 0.0009355545043945312, -0.0280303955078125, 0.0189666748046875, 0.0124359130859375, -0.0272064208984375, 0.0016164779663085938, 0.01226806640625, -0.005947113037109375, 0.01715087890625, 0.0141143798828125, 0.0009002685546875, -0.01145172119140625, -0.00862884521484375, -0.00039768218994140625, -0.016693115234375, -0.0279541015625, -0.0276031494140625, -0.0196533203125, -0.0226593017578125, -0.0010585784912109375, 0.029052734375, -0.01424407958984375, 0.01580810546875, 0.0322265625, -0.036956787109375, 0.041046142578125, -0.02642822265625, 0.0214691162109375, -0.0190277099609375, 0.01141357421875, 8.124113082885742e-05, -0.00952911376953125, 0.0284423828125, 0.006191253662109375, -0.01580810546875, 0.0142974853515625, -0.0323486328125, 0.017120361328125, 0.00669097900390625, -0.0113372802734375, 0.00021648406982421875, 0.01415252685546875, 0.0474853515625, 0.0137176513671875, -0.0184326171875, -0.0205230712890625, 0.009918212890625, 0.01922607421875, 0.018218994140625, 0.00371551513671875, 0.029449462890625, -0.000164031982421875, 0.01373291015625, -0.00418853759765625, 0.0170440673828125, 0.005809783935546875, 0.0266571044921875, -0.01495361328125, -0.0173187255859375, 0.006755828857421875, -0.01107025146484375, -0.0178680419921875, -0.004444122314453125, 0.02056884765625, -9.822845458984375e-05, 0.01715087890625, -0.00930023193359375, 0.007236480712890625, 0.02740478515625, 0.032379150390625, 0.0078582763671875, -0.0208740234375, 0.00786590576171875, 0.00452423095703125, 0.0234527587890625, -0.0098876953125, 0.01393890380859375, -0.004856109619140625, -0.0227203369140625, 0.0057373046875, -0.0142974853515625, -0.0019969940185546875, -0.006259918212890625, 0.044281005859375, 0.026123046875, -0.0039215087890625, -0.0272064208984375, -0.0016508102416992188, 0.01190948486328125, 0.0006489753723144531, 0.006626129150390625, 0.00970458984375, -0.0208587646484375, 0.00807952880859375, -0.01788330078125, -0.0209503173828125, 0.0011749267578125, -0.024200439453125, 0.01715087890625, -0.055267333984375, 0.01227569580078125, -0.0160064697265625, -0.01445770263671875, 0.019134521484375, -0.0302581787109375, -0.005229949951171875, -0.01512908935546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally learn from a stream of tasks and update a textual memory bank (a persistent store of natural language summaries and feedback appended after each episode) to improve over time\u2014have demonstrated strong task performance. However, their *reliability aspects*, specifically the consistency and robustness of their performance across sources of stochasticity (such as random seeds, sampling, or agent initialization) and variable task sequences, remain underexplored. Here, we define *consistency* as the degree of low between-run variance in agent performance under identical settings, and *robustness* as the agent's performance stability when facing changes in task ordering and stochastic elements. We further operationalize *implicit curriculum* as any inadvertent sequencing of tasks such that the agent is exposed to easier instances before harder ones, thereby smoothing the learning process (cf. Bengio et al., 2009), and *memory drift* as the measurable accumulation of errors or irrelevant/incorrect memory entries that misguide future behavior, visible through increased frequency of policy failures or non-productive actions over time.\n\nTo ground the reliability challenges of these agents, we systematically address three research questions:\n(1) How consistent is the performance of memory-based self-improving agents under different runs and task orderings?\n(2) What are the main sources and error modes of fragility or instability in these agents' improvement processes?\n(3) Can explicit specification and feedback mitigate these weaknesses, and what are the limitations of such interventions?\n\n**Experimental Setup and Environments**\n\nWe re-evaluate two representative memory-based methods: (a) episodic memory augmentation for language agents (agents appending text summaries of actions, errors, and corrections to persistent memory, based on [Wang et al., 2023]); and (b) reinforcement learning (RL) agents retrieving and querying a textual archive of past interactions\u2014state, action, reward, and feedback traces\u2014to inform policy updates (as in [Lee et al., 2022]). Both agent types employ a transformer backbone (6 layers, 512 hidden units) and a sliding-window memory buffer with the 100 most-recent entries.\n\n**Task Environments:**  \n- *Procedural tasks:* In a 5\u00d75 grid-world, agents must execute a fixed English instruction such as \"Move the red block onto the blue square then ring the bell,\" requiring navigation, object manipulation, and sequential tool use. Success requires correct subtask execution in order.\n- *Reasoning tasks:* Agents are given word problems like \"If you start with 3 apples and double the number, then subtract 2, how many are left?\"\u2014requiring 4-step arithmetic or symbolic manipulations.\nTask instances are algorithmically generated to cover varying difficulty. Complete environment code and reward structure are shared in Supplementary Materials.\n\n**Evaluation Criteria:**  \nWe report:  \n(i) *Task completion rate*\u2014fraction of episodes that reach the goal state as defined by environment-specific criteria;  \n(ii) *Average episode reward*\u2014the cumulative numeric reward per episode based on subgoal progress.  \nEach configuration is run for 200 episodes, with ten independent runs per agent/method. Task order is randomized for each run (uniform sampling without replacement), unless otherwise specified, thus removing implicit curricula unless constructed for experiment.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Performance Variance and Task Order Sensitivity*\n\nAcross random seeds and task orders, performance is highly variable. For procedural tasks, completion rates range from 55% to 88% (mean 68%, SD 13.7%, Cohen's d \u2248 1.2 for best/worst runs), and for reasoning tasks from 41% to 73% (mean 56%, SD 11.2%). These results are statistically significant (Welch\u2019s t-test, p<0.01). Importantly, this variance exceeds thresholds tolerable for many real-world applications, as a failure rate swing of 30%\u201340% would make results unreliable in operational deployments. The *self-improving loop* amplifies this unpredictability: since each episode's memory contributes to subsequent policy decisions, errors can snowball, as seen in Table 1. In about 28% of procedural and 34% of reasoning runs, a single misinterpretation early in memory propagates, manifesting as extended error streaks or repeated suboptimal actions, quantifying the operational impact of *memory drift*.\n\nImprovement trajectories are extremely sensitive to task order. With randomized task order, mean completion gains drop by 10\u201318% (95% CI: 8.1\u201319.0%) relative to fixed ascending-difficulty curricula. In 30% of shuffled runs, improvement completely stalls (completion plateaus at or below baseline). Notably, constructing a deliberately *adversarial ordering*\u2014where the hardest tasks appear first and interleave distractor or misleading instances\u2014can further depress performance (average completion drops 17\u201324% below curriculum baseline), quantifying the significance of implicit curriculum effects (see Table 2, and discussion in [Hosseini et al., 2021]).\n\n**Diagnosis of Failure Modes and Memory Drift**\n\nManual and quantitative trace analyses reveal error-prone or ambiguous feedback as chief drivers of *memory drift*. Labelling 200 randomly-sampled memory entries across runs, we find that on average 32% are ambiguous, off-task, or cumulatively misleading in failed runs (vs. 9% in successful runs). Frequent errors include: storing incomplete subgoal results (18% of entries), failure misattribution (12%), and language drift (e.g., references that lose relevance as the state changes, 7%). Representative example (reasoning task): agent incorrectly stores \"doubled apples to 6,\" overlooking an intervening subtraction step, which leads to repeated miscalculations in future tasks.\n\n**Interventions: Structured Rubrics and Granular Feedback**\n\nTo mitigate these issues, we modified memory construction via:  \n(i) *Structured rubrics*\u2014explicit checklists detailing required subgoals (\"Red block on blue square: Yes/No; Bell rung: Yes/No\") appended to each memory entry, clarifying task criteria.  \n(ii) *Granular programmatic feedback*\u2014task environment marks each subtask as passed/failed post-episode, rather than only reporting a binary outcome.\n\nWith these additions, worst-case completion rates rose from 55% to 63% (procedural) and 41% to 49% (reasoning) (improvements 5\u20138%, p<0.05; paired t-test, Cohen\u2019s d \u2248 0.34). Standard deviation dropped by 2\u20134%. Notably, while frequency of ambiguous memory entries fell by 40%, between-run variance persisted (range 14\u201319%), indicating that while interventions curbed some *memory drift* and misinterpretations, underlying sensitivity to ordering and error propagation remain.  \n*Limitations:* These interventions require increased annotation or engineering effort, as task-specific rubrics and feedback generation are non-trivial to design and may increase annotation costs by 15\u201320%. Moreover, pilot adversarial tests reveal a risk of overfitting: agents may optimize exclusively for rubric cues, neglecting broader generalization (e.g., failing when rubric wording is perturbed).\n\n**Transition: Synthesis of Findings with Stated Research Questions**\n\nThe above experiments yield the following answers:\n(1) *Consistency under different runs and orderings:* Performance exhibits high, practically significant variance across seeds and orderings; mean-only reporting obscures frequent catastrophic failures.\n(2) *Major fragility sources:* Systematic error accumulation through the self-improving/memory drift loop and pronounced sensitivity to task sequence (implicit/explicit curriculum effects), with evidence that ambiguity and feedback underspecification directly raise error frequencies.\n(3) *Explicit specification and feedback:* Structured rubrics and granular feedback improve reliability and reduce error propagation, but gains are modest, come at annotation/engineering cost, and do not eliminate sensitivity to task sequence. Automating rubric generation and exploring more robust feedback representations remain open problems.\n\n**Comparison with Prior Work and Unique Contributions**\n\nPrior work ([Wang et al., 2023]; [Lee et al., 2022]) emphasizes mean performance under fixed curricula\u2014often omitting variance analysis and underestimating curriculum effects. Reviews ([Gao et al., 2023]; [Hosseini et al., 2021]) theorize that fixed orderings can inflate apparent agent capability by smoothing learning. Our results systematically quantify these effects: (a) establishing high between-run variance as a critical obstacle, (b) pinpointing curriculum-induced overestimation of robustness, and (c) analyzing memory drift using coded error metrics. These contributions move beyond anecdotal reporting, providing clear metrics and experimental stress tests that explicitly measure reliability threats and mitigation effects\u2014enabling practitioners and researchers to meaningfully compare agent stability under varied, representative scenarios.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\n- (1) Evaluate self-improving agents by reporting both mean and variance of completion and reward metrics, across at least ten random seeds and fully randomized (non-curriculum) task orders.  \n- (2) Construct *adversarial orderings* (e.g., hardest tasks or distractors first, random segments of mixed-difficulty tasks specifically designed to disrupt incremental learning) to probe agents' resilience.\n- (3) Implement interface standards supporting *structured rubrics* (explicit, task-decomposed checklists) and programmatic feedback (granular pass/fail markers for all subtasks or predicates)\u2014for both agent learning and external diagnostics. Where possible, automate rubric or feedback generation to reduce manual cost; report full success/failure distributions by error type.\n  \nWe hope these concrete, operationalized recommendations\u2014grounded in our findings\u2014will help advance the development and evaluation of robust, transparent, and trustworthy memory-based self-improving agents."}}